<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Selim I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1470">1470</a>–<a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Selim I was Ottoman sultan from 1512 to 1520. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> at Chaldiran in 1514 and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in 1516–1517, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the holy cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> under Ottoman rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and Mamluk Sultanate profiles note that his conquest ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> state and the shadow Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selim-i.jpg" alt="Selim I" width="253" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Naḳḳāş ʿOs̠mān · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nakka%C5%9F_Selim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Selim was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a> on 10 October 1470, the son of Şehzade Bayezid, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a>, and Gülbahar Hatun, a Pontic Greek concubine. In 1487 his father sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> as governor. There he faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> on the eastern frontier. In 1505 a Safavid prince, Ebrahim Mirza, marched on Trabzon with 3,000 men, and Selim repelled him with an expeditionary force of 450 and chased him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>. In 1507 Selim attacked Erzincan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayburt">Bayburt</a> and defeated a Safavid army of 10,000, at a time when his father had not answered Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>'s violations of Ottoman sovereignty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Selim's early reign was dominated by tension with Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, who had made Twelver Shia Islam the state religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and by 1510 held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In 1511 Ismail supported the Şahkulu Rebellion in Anatolia, and Selim's mufti issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> of takfir against him and his followers. Selim had Shiites aged 7 to 70 listed in central Anatolian cities including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a>, and his forces executed those they found, most by beheading, in what was the largest massacre in Ottoman history until the end of the 19th century. During the advance of 1514 his troops suffered from Ismail's scorched-earth tactics.</p><p>In 1516 Selim attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> and defeated it at Marj Dabiq on 24 August 1516 and at Ridaniya on 22 January 1517. He annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and with them the holy cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. He took the title Servant of the Two Holy Cities. The last Abbasid caliph, al-Mutawakkil III, was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. An account of a formal transfer of the caliphate to Selim emerged in the eighteenth century, but it was a later invention, and Selim himself made no claim to the office. The conquest ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-mamluk-naval-war">Portuguese–Mamluk naval war</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took over attempts to check Portuguese expansion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Selim was planning a campaign westward when he fell ill and died on 22 September 1520, aged 49. The official cause was a mistreated carbuncle, though some historians suggest cancer or poison, and others note that plague was then present in the empire. He was buried in what became known as the Yavuz Selim Mosque, commissioned by his son Suleiman I.</p><p>He was bilingual in Turkish and Persian and wrote poetry in both under the pen name Selimi. He had a fiery temper, and a popular Ottoman curse wished that one might be a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of Selim's. Many historians hold that he prepared the empire for its height under Suleiman I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Selim I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defeat at the Battle of Marj Dabiq in 1516, where Sultan Qansuh al-Ghawri was killed, and at Ridaniya near Cairo in 1517 led to the Ottoman conquest of Egypt under <b>Selim I</b>, ending Mamluk sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Selim I</b>’s conquest of the Mamluk Sultanate in 1516–1517 brought Egypt, Syria and the holy cities of Mecca and Medina under Ottoman sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His brother Hayreddin Barbarossa, facing Spanish counter-attacks and local rivals, offered Algiers to the Ottoman Sultan <b>Selim I</b> around 1519.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>There Sultan <b>Selim I</b> deployed artillery and musket-armed janissary infantry against the Qizilbash cavalry, whose leaders, by tradition, scorned firearms as unmanly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1800 the Arabic-speaking lands from Iraq to Algeria were, at least in name, provinces of the Ottoman Empire, which had ruled Syria and Egypt since the conquests of <b>Selim I</b> in 1516–1517.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Selim I</b> conquered the Mamluk Sultanate in 1516–1517, the sultans also claimed the title of caliph.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Çorlu, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.65,35.83&amp;year=1502" aria-label="Amasya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5429 712 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Selim I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5879 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5879 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.65,35.83&amp;year=1502">What was here in 1502? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
